SM(220,{"n":"Genesis 1.9-31, 2.1-3","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Genesis 1.9-31, 2.1-3.mp3","d":3119.9,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.48,"Verse 9. Then God said, Let the water under heaven be gathered together into one place, and let the dry land appear."],[33.84,"And it was so."],[35.12,"The water under heaven was gathered into its places, and the dry land appeared."],[40.56,"So God called the dry land earth, and the gathering together of the waters he called seas, and God saw that it was good."],[51.28,"Then God said, Let the earth bring forth the herb of grass, bearing seed according to its kind and likeness."],[57.68,"Let the fruit tree bear fruit whose seed is in itself according to its kind on earth."],[62.6,"It was so."],[64.04,"Thus the earth brought forth the herb of grass bearing seed according to its kind and likeness."],[69.56,"The fruit trees bore fruit, whose seed is in itself according to its kind on earth."],[75.56,"God saw that it was good."],[77.16,"So evening and morning were the third day."],[89.64,"And notice the language."],[92.41,"God doesn't cause the dry land to like rise up out of the water."],[97.37,"That'd be right."],[98.17,"If we were making a movie today, that's probably how we'd depict it."],[101.34,"Like rise out of the water."],[102.78,"What it says is that God takes the waters and sort of pulls them back and restrains them, gathers them together so that the dry land appears."],[114.75,"Why is that?"],[115.79,"Because the seas again represent chaos."],[119.65,"We started out with waters, with chaos."],[123.17,"So God is taking that chaos and restraining it."],[128.55,"To produce order."],[130.15,"So God is the one who's sort of holding the chaos that's out at the extremes back from the earth that he's put in order."],[144.31,"And this is there's also then this transition."],[146.47,"Because he's now created again spaces"],[150.41,"He's created the uh the dry land."],[155.21,"And so we start to see the beginnings of life."],[158.59,"Because we get plants, and we get trees that bear fruit."],[163.32,"Fruit by definition has seeds in it."],[166.14,"It's a plant ovum, essentially."],[170.62,"Um that it can reproduce."],[175.1,"So these first three days are focused on the uh that first issue of disorder, of chaos."],[185.98,"And God is putting things in order and creating these spaces. which are still mostly empty"],[195.54,"Now we're going to see as we move to the second set of three days, those three days are going to be in parallel with the first three days."],[205.06,"But they're going to be answering the second problem."],[209.48,"The emptiness. The emptiness."],[214.3,"And this, the fact that this is in two sets of two parallel sets of three days is treated as important, not just by the fathers, but even in our liturgical tradition."],[227.82,"One of the readings for Holy Saturday, before Pos.ca is from Genesis chapter one"],[237.38,"And you read these first three days."],[244.98,"And you think, well, why don't you read?"],[247.54,"Why do you stop halfway through the story?"],[250.47,"And that's connected to Christ's rising on the third day."],[256.87,"So there are going to be other things about Christ related to the Sabbath that we'll get to when we get to the end of the chapter."],[266.27,"But there is also this connection of the restoration of order."],[274.99,"Because remember, we talked about how that order is later going to be called justice."],[277.07,"St Paul is going to say in Romans that Christ was raised for our justification."],[289.03,"Be justified means to be put back in order."],[295.83,"So putting that reading there connects the three days of the third day of Christ's resurrection to Christ restoring the order of creation."],[306.95,"As Christ said in Revelation, making all things new."],[315.99,"Okay, so now we'll start day four."],[318.87,"Now we're getting into we're putting things in order."],[321.91,"we're Now going to be filling things with life."],[326.03,"Verse 14. Then God said, Let there be lights in the firmament of heaven for illumination to divide day from night."],[335.31,"This is what I meant by if you try to take both the fourth day and the first day super literally, you're going to run into problems."],[350.43,"Let them be for illumination in the firmament of heaven to give light on the earth."],[354.83,"It was so."],[356.59,"Then God made two great lights, the greater light to rule the day, and the lesser light to rule the night."],[362.35,"He made the stars also."],[364.75,"God set them in the firmament of heaven to give light lig.ht on the earth and to rule over the day and over the night, and to divide the from the darkness"],[374.07,"God saw that it was good."],[376.23,"So evening and morning were the fourth day."],[382.15,"So the sun, moon, and stars are created."],[384.95,"We're not used to thinking of them as life."],[390.25,"Especially as modern people."],[392.57,"We're used to thinking of the sun as a mass of incandescent gas."],[398.73,"Gigantic nuclear furnace."],[401.13,"Where hydrogen is turned into helium."],[403.69,"Anyway. Um And we're used to thinking of the moon as a big rock."],[413.19,"That orbits around the earth."],[414.79,"Um, and the stars as being like the sun, just really far away, uh, and the planets as being like the moon just farther away."],[425.16,"Um, but we have to understand that for ancient people, the sun, moon, and stars weren't just a representation of divine beings, but they were those divine beings."],[442.84,"And so, in the case of the scriptures, they are seen as being angelic beings."],[454.65,"So we we saw all through Revelation, angels are stars all the time"],[462.37,"It's a direct comparison."],[464.21,"But um so the understanding here, and and the place where you really see that."],[471.14,"So they could be, you know, balls of plasma, and rocks, and help you divide seasons and times and years."],[479.86,"But it's the ruling over aspect, ruling over the day and ruling over the night."],[488.1,"And so the idea here, this is why a lot of the fathers connect this fourth day to the creation of the angels."],[494.08,"Because they're making this direct connection."],[497.24,"And because the way they understood angels to function is that as Philo, who's a Jewish writer in the first century BC, put it, uh, they're not gods."],[511.49,"But they're angelic beings who serve God."],[514.61,"And uh God has an administration."],[520.34,"He's the king, but the king has a court."],[524.79,"And the uh these angelic beings are his administration."],[529.75,"And so God assigns them to different parts of his creation to oversee those things, to guide those things."],[539.51,"And that that's what's being brought out here"],[544.8,"We're gonna see this later in the Torah."],[546.56,"It's gonna talk about the sun, moon, and stars to whom God assigned the governance of the nations other than Israel in Deuteronomy 4."],[554.16,"It's gonna be like, what?"],[556.0,"Um this is also reflected in our iconography, where the sun and moon are frequently depicted with faces."],[565.68,"Um and we talk about like in the time of Christ's crucifixion, the sun and moon hiding their faces."],[574.58,"This is that connection to angelic beings."],[578.5,"And as we've talked about going through the New Testament and connecting that with the promises to Abraham, the promise in the Old Testament is that someday the righteous will shine like stars."],[589.26,"And the whole idea of sainthood is that humans who are glorified, who find salvation, also take up places in that administration."],[600.23,"So God doesn't need anybody to help him administer the cosmos."],[603.59,"It's not like, oh, it's too big for him to handle."],[606.79,"So he needs a bureaucracy."],[608.71,"It's God out of love shares his life and his rule over creation with his creatures."],[618.07,"And that includes the angelic beings."],[622.81,"So these are very much."],[626.41,"right We talk about the light and and the darkness, the day the night, the heavens."],[627.59,"the These are creatures who live there."],[633.92,"Even though we're not used to thinking of them as alive at all."],[639.41,"So, but you can see how day four and day one, are parallel."],[644.16,"Dealing with these two issues."],[647.36,"So now, day five."],[650.32,"Then God said, Let the waters bring forth creatures having life."],[655.6,"And let birds fly above the earth across the face of heaven's firmament."],[660.08,"It was so."],[661.36,"Thus God made great sea creatures, and every living thing that moves, with which the waters abounded according to their kind, and every winged bird according to its kind."],[672.04,"God saw that it was good."],[674.2,"God blessed them, saying, Be fruitful and multiply, and fill the waters and the seas, and let birds multiply on earth."],[681.8,"So evening and morning were the fifth day."],[685.16,"So you can see right off the bat, second day, fifth day."],[689.18,"Second day, God separates the sky and the sea."],[692.78,"Fifth day he fills the sea and he fills the skies with life."],[697.82,"And tells them to continue."],[699.92,"Be fruitful and multiply it."],[701.44,"Fill it. Now it makes it more sense than birds coming out of the sea from. the case"],[707.04,"Yeah. As opposed to that"],[709.76,"Right. So now that those two spaces. are filled with life"],[715.89,"Another interesting thing to note in the Hebrew here is that um it's translated here as uh every living thing that moves."],[726.85,"Uh what it literally says in the Hebrew is every soul."],[732.53,"So we have this uh kind of platonic thing that we've gotten for Western culture that humans have souls and nothing else does."],[744.13,"But uh no ancient people thought that."],[748.21,"Ancient people thought that having a soul is what made something alive as opposed to inanimate."],[757.25,"And I used that word on purpose because anima is the Latin word for soul."],[758.45,"So inanimate literally means soulless"],[766.06,"Um but different living things have different types of souls."],[771.9,"And as late as St."],[773.02,"Gregory Palamos, he has a he has a treatise on the souls of animals."],[777.82,"Yes. Yes, which is why why my dogs could be heathens."],[784.46,"Um but um he says that of course they have souls."],[789.86,"They're alive, but it's a different kind of soul than a human soul."],[796.51,"Um, and so that's just a very common way of speaking."],[802.84,"Um, but they didn't translate it literally here, because people have been like, what?"],[807.64,"All the souls that swim in the waters?"],[809.4,"What are you doing?"],[810.53,"But the idea they translated as living things, because that's pretty much what it means."],[814.77,"Like having a soul means you're alive."],[822.34,"Verse 24. Then God said, Let the earth bring forth the living creatures according to its kind, the quadrupeds."],[831.22,"There's a great translation."],[832.74,"The creeping things and the wild animals of the earth according to their kind."],[837.54,"It was so."],[838.98,"So God made the wild animals of the earth according to their kind, the cattle according to their kind, and all the creeping things on earth according to their kind."],[847.5,"God saw that it was good."],[850.14,"Then God said, Let us well, we'll pause there."],[853.98,"Before we get to humans, we'll put we'll pause."],[856.47,"So now, again, right away you can see."],[858.71,"Third day, he made the dry land."],[861.99,"Sixth day. All the living creatures that live on the land, he's filling it with life."],[867.15,"So we have this uh parallel again."],[871.44,"And uh I'll comment here on the refrain that we've seen on all of these days, where it said, God saw that it was good."],[883.12,"Um good gets used in a lot of ways in English today."],[889.69,"So we use good as opposed to bad or as opposed to evil."],[896.41,"We use good as a mild uh as a form of mild praise."],[906.89,"That tastes good."],[907.66,"Yeah, that tastes good."],[909.9,"It's pretty good."],[911.52,"Um, there's a place not far from my house that has on their permanent sign home of good boudin."],[921.04,"And I'm like, well, that's a modest claim."],[923.04,"Like, they're not saying it's great, they're not saying it's the best in town, but it's it's good."],[928.53,"Um, and uh none of those that I just mentioned are what the word good is getting at here."],[934.97,"So Hebrew it's the word tov."],[937.29,"And good really means something more like beautiful or whole."],[945.26,"Beautiful or whole, and that beauty element gets at that aspect of order."],[953.74,"Not just on the first first three days, but you notice on days five and six, the animals are brought out according to their kinds."],[966.44,"Which there's there's an interesting juxtaposition there in terms of the original concept."],[972.86,"So you have these sea creatures."],[973.66,"Remember the sea is chaos"],[976.74,"Sea creatures, including great sea like sea monsters, according to their kind."],[986.15,"And on land, wild animals and creeping things, which is basically reptiles."],[991.83,"It's herpetone in uh Greek."],[995.29,"According to their kind."],[997.53,"So these animals that were considered unclean animals, these animals that were seen as sort of indicative of chaos, are according to their kind."],[1009.07,"So even those things are part of God's order in the original creation."],[1016.35,"They're all part of that"],[1019.91,"And so they can be called good in the sense of beauty."],[1025.44,"And wholeness. Because of that order."],[1032.64,"So now we'll do humans."],[1039.6,"Verse 26, then God said, Let us make man in our image according to our likeness."],[1045.2,"Let them have dominion over the fish of the sea, over the birds of heaven, over the cattle and over all the earth, and over every creeping thing that moves on the earth."],[1055.92,"So God made man, in the image of God, he made him, male and female, he made them."],[1062.28,"Then God blessed them, and God said to them, Be fruitful and multiply, fill the earth and subdue it, and have dominion over the fish of the sea, over the birds of heaven, and over every living thing that moves on the earth."],[1077.16,"So a bunch of things there, but we'll go through step by step."],[1083.56,"So uh here's another place I'm probably gonna get accused of heresy."],[1088.36,"Because I know a lot of people really like to use the let us here."],[1095.0,"Really want to connect that to the Trinity."],[1100.97,"Trinity's here. But this is not the place."],[1104.55,"That's the grammatical part"],[1106.51,"I'll get into that in a middle minute."],[1109.95,"If you're looking for the Trinity in Genesis 1, we've already seen it."],[1115.31,"Because we have God creating."],[1119.15,"We have the Spirit of God hovering over the waters."],[1123.15,"And what does he use to create?"],[1127.71,"Word. His word."],[1129.47,"He says, let there be light."],[1131.09,"He creates through."],[1134.47,"W wordord, very literally."],[1136.39,"That's the place where we see."],[1138.88,"God's act of creation happens through the word and the spirit."],[1143.78,"That's the place where we see the Trinity here."],[1145.62,"All things were made through him"],[1153.04,"Right. So he's coming back and he's interpreting the drawing this out."],[1158.42,"And then the word becomes flesh."],[1160.28,"And is Christ."],[1161.36,"Um that's there."],[1166.08,"So uh Elohim, which is what's being translated as God, all through here, is grammatically plural."],[1178.64,"Uh you will sometimes hear people say that this is a plural of majesty."],[1187.6,"And they will say that because they're trying to connect it to the royal we."],[1190.88,"To Queen Victoria saying, we are not amused"],[1196.66,"Which I don't know that she ever actually said, but in every movie she says that at some point."],[1204.64,"They're trying to connect it to that."],[1206.88,"That's not totally off base, but it's also not right."],[1210.8,"Because Hebrew doesn't actually have a plural of majesty."],[1214.96,"But uh it's kind of similar."],[1218.0,"Hebrew does have a plural of intensification."],[1223.76,"Where you put something in plural to intensify, in the sense that, so for example."],[1233.6,"When we would say, like the mo.untain of mountains in English, or the great mountain"],[1241.16,"You could do that in Hebrew by just making the word plural."],[1245.56,"You just say harim."],[1248.68,"When referring to one mountain."],[1250.36,"You call it harim in the plural to say this is the great mountain."],[1253.72,"This is the mountain of mountains."],[1257.24,"You could just say like kings instead of king of kings."],[1260.9,"And have the same effect."],[1262.28,"When you're referring to one person."],[1265.0,"Another biblical example of this is behemoth."],[1269.09,"Behemoth is a grammatical plural."],[1271.33,"Behemot. It's actually the plural of the word for cow or bull."],[1277.81,"Behemoth Yeah. Super cow."],[1283.61,"It's sort of the, or it can be used for a beast."],[1286.09,"So it's sort of the super beast or the beast of beasts."],[1288.99,"We talked about that a little in Revelation when we were talking about the beasts."],[1291.85,"Behemoth. This idea of the beast of beasts."],[1294.25,"And you just put it in plural to say that."],[1296.67,"You're pointing at this one beast and calling it catam, essentially, if you're really literal."],[1302.67,"But the point being it's an intensifier."],[1305.15,"And so that seems to be what's going on with Elohim."],[1311.4,"But it's not just like, hey, here's a convenient way to say that the god we're talking about is God capital G."],[1320.45,"The God of gods."],[1322.07,"Uh, the great God, God most high."],[1325.72,"Um there's an extra reason to do that because the singular L, it also means God, but L singular was also the name of a particular pagan god, the father of Baal."],[1345.29,"So if the Torah had used the singular L."],[1351.31,"In the beginning, L created heavens and the earth, the Canaanites would have been like, oh, okay."],[1358.27,"When does he, you know, take a wife and give birth to Baal."],[1362.55,"Like that's's so it also a way of distinguishing the God who we're talking about from that pagan God."],[1370.85,"We're not talking about him."],[1372.45,"We're talking about God capital G."],[1375.27,"The most high God."],[1377.29,"Not that dude."],[1378.97,"And he was something so better than L."],[1381.85,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[1384.65,"Even though it means what?"],[1387.21,"Plural, yeah. Yeah, yeah."],[1391.05,"Um and so, because it's in the plural, you sometimes find plural pronouns, plural verbs, like let us. right connect it to it"],[1412.18,"So I believe in the Trinity."],[1414.51,"I'm just saying pointing to this is a bad argument for it."],[1418.45,"For the for the doctrine of the Trinity."],[1420.21,"That's not going to convince much of anybody."],[1425.06,"So, yeah, don't try to take that too far."],[1429.14,"But there are things to point to here, like we talked about."],[1431.8,"God creating through his word, the pres and through his spirit."],[1435.34,"There are things to point here to here if you want to talk about the Trinity, connecting it to St."],[1439.66,"John's Gospel in the beginning of it."],[1445.74,"So then he says, let's make men in our our image according to likeness."],[1451.94,"And then in verse 27, God does this."],[1455.39,"Made man in the image of God."],[1458.91,"So what does that mean?"],[1463.79,"How long could we all want to be here?"],[1465.71,"No. So the um we tend to think of us being in the image of God as a static concept."],[1479.2,"Um now we don't take it so woodenly literally."],[1483.3,"Like image like photo."],[1485.0,"Like we look like God."],[1486.2,"Unless you're Kenneth Copeland."],[1488.04,"Um that was a cheap shot."],[1490.64,"But anyway. Um."],[1493.14,"So it doesn't mean that we physically look like that's God, that's not what it's getting at."],[1500.54,"Um, but the more common way to take it as sort of a static idea, uh a single category, is people will try to identify identify some facet of human nature and say, well, that's what the image of God is."],[1519.07,"That is super problematic."],[1522.19,"First of all, a bunch of them don't work out."],[1524.91,"So you'll find a lot of theologians suggesting that like language."],[1530.69,"And that's an interesting suggestion."],[1532.55,"Because Christ is the word."],[1534.19,"So you want to do language."],[1535.79,"But then we discover that like lots of animals have language."],[1542.43,"So that doesn't work out so well."],[1544.91,"The most common thing, and this is coming from Aristotle more than the Bible, is reason."],[1554.88,"Why does that cause a problem?"],[1557.04,"Well, does that mean that an unborn infant isn't in the image of God?"],[1565.84,"Because it doesn't have the capacity to reason."],[1570.48,"Does that mean someone with advanced dementia or someone who suffered a brain injury is no longer a human in the image of God because they don't have reason?"],[1583.16,"And so any attempt to kind of just nail this down as oh, this is some faculty that humans possess can not only doesn't work out so well, but can be dangerous morally."],[1596.64,"And ethically. And not just can hypothetically be, but has been in human history."],[1606.56,"So the concept here is much more dynamic than static."],[1616.32,"It has to do with a capacity."],[1620.34,"A potential that human beings have."],[1625.58,"Imaging God is something that we can actively do or not do."],[1632.86,"Functioning as God's image is something we can do or not do."],[1658.54,"This includes the relationship between God the Father and Christ."],[1663.66,"We're told in the New Testament that Christ is the express image."],[1668.44,"The perfect image, the complete image of God the Father."],[1674.92,"But again, going to St."],[1676.28,"John's Gospel, what that means is, as Christ says in St."],[1679.56,"John's Gospel, that means the things he says are the things that the Father says."],[1685.16,"The things he does are the things the Father does."],[1692.2,"And so when someone who knew my dad says I'm the spitting image of my dad."],[1699.76,"Sometimes they just mean my physical appearance."],[1704.54,"But more often it's something about the way I talk, some mannerism I have, some way I express myself."],[1715.12,"That reminds them."],[1717.68,"And so this is why here we have this in the image and likeness."],[1723.68,"And the Hebrew words and the Greek words there are basically synonyms."],[1728.88,"But the fathers make a distinction between the two."],[1732.48,"They're not making a grammatical distinction."],[1734.72,"They are synonyms."],[1735.68,"They do mean the same thing."],[1737.84,"But the distinction they're making is image being this potential we have."],[1748.72,"To be imitators of God, to be participants."],[1751.53,"To be his image in the world."],[1753.05,"in the world. And reflect him"],[1757.15,"And likeness is the end, the goal of that."],[1763.95,"The final fulfillment of that that we aim toward."],[1769.97,"The sort of the fullness of the image being the likeness."],[1773.43,"That idea. So that through through being God's image in the world, we become like him."],[1791.57,"At the end."],[1794.45,"So image is sort of both somet.hing we're born possessing in that potential"],[1803.53,"We have the ability to become like God."],[1807.37,"As uh St."],[1808.73,"Gregory the theologian said, uh, humans are the being who are commanded to become God."],[1817.82,"And likeness, the image, because it means the same thing, is also the end, the goal."],[1824.56,"At the end."],[1826.0,"And that destiny and that potentiality is what separates humanity from other creatures."],[1833.92,"Not just animals, but angels."],[1842.41,"And that's fulfilled in Christ."],[1844.59,"Because in Christ, humanity and God are perfectly united."],[1854.35,"Christ was not incarnate as a dog or a sheep or an angel."],[1861.15,"But as our human nature."],[1864.67,"And that's what gives us this potential and this destiny."],[1877.47,"So that's men and women."],[1879.12,"Well, that's what I'm about to get to."],[1880.88,"This is also the very this, and this is very important."],[1884.25,"Because we're gonna read about the cre the creation of man and woman in chapter two."],[1891.13,"But this gets brought in here, this male and female, he created them for a very important reason."],[1899.18,"So this seems obvious to us now."],[1905.15,"But uh for most of human history, this was radical to the point of being revolutionary."],[1918.27,"Um if we're talking about the ancient Near East, you have a hard time finding anyone who thinks women are human."],[1928.63,"By the time you get to Aristotle in Greece in the 4th century BC, he thinks that women are defective men."],[1939.67,"He literally taught that all fetuses are developing into men, and some of them don't quite get there and are born as women."],[1949.67,"So for Aristotle, being a woman is a birth defect."],[1954.15,"Essentially. And that's an improvement over a lot of the other cultures at the time."],[1962.75,"You know, for Plato, if you read the symposium."],[1965.33,"It's impossible for a man to love a woman because a man can only love his equal."],[1970.97,"Other delightful things. like this"],[1974.69,"So I mean, this is the reality of the whole ancient world."],[1978.06,"And this isn't a New Testament thing."],[1980.86,"This is the Torah."],[1982.4,"Already has this."],[1984.34,"That are placing male humans and female humans on an equal plane, specifically in terms of this potential and this destiny."],[1999.7,"In terms of serving as the image of God."],[2004.1,"Which is was unheard of."],[2007.94,"So where did that evolve, I guess?"],[2013.54,"Yeahah, ye. yeah. Oh"],[2014.46,"I understood it."],[2015.26,"There was a Jewish saying, thank God I'm not involved."],[2021.34,"Wait, they were tweaking."],[2024.7,"A woman. Yeah."],[2025.66,"Gentile. Gentile. Who are all pretty much dogs anyway, yeah."],[2031.82,"Um. Well, that's later, but you do find stuff like that."],[2036.62,"Uh you find um one of the most uh Pen Syrah that's just straight out says at one point, women are evil."],[2051.42,"Um he's trying to do a thing with Eve."],[2055.52,"He's basically trying to give Adam's excuse, which we'll see in Genesis 3."],[2059.2,"Adam's excuse didn't get him very far with God."],[2061.82,"When God comes to question him, he's like, it's not me, it's this woman you put here with me."],[2066.51,"But Ben Zirach was still trying to use that."],[2070.51,"St. Paul kind of responds to that."],[2073.63,"In Ephesians. Yeah."],[2089.15,"That's a nice read."],[2090.59,"Yeah. So, but no, but this is this is important because the reality is the rest of the Old Testament is about Israel's failure to keep the Torah."],[2108.53,"That's the story of the Old Testament is God gave the Torah, they didn't keep it."],[2116.39,"That's that's it."],[2117.59,"And that's where St."],[2118.55,"Paul goes, like in Romans 2."],[2121.93,"He says there are great advantages to being one of the Jewish people, like you have the Torah."],[2127.29,"Of course, do you keep the Torah?"],[2130.14,"Because if you don't keep it, it's not a big advantage."],[2133.39,"Um yeah, that's so the the it is very often posed in our contemporary world, that if if uh ancient Israel Second Temple Jewish people Christians haven't lived up to what's in the Bible then people will try to well then the Bible must not actually say that it's like no no no no you don't decide what the Bible says or what Christianity teaches based on what we've successfully pulled off right as a mass group right because they're not talking about individual saints who may have right they're talking about people like me right who don't right um you look at what it says."],[2182.55,"I mean, I mean, there are worse things to be than a hypocrite, because at least as a hypocrite you have standards."],[2188.44,"Like you have something you' youre not're not that you believe that living up to."],[2190.08,"that Like's better than it's better to be a hypocrite than a heretic."],[2196.9,"You can quote me on that."],[2200.74,"Because at least you're pursuing the right goal, you're just falling short."],[2205.31,"And one of the beautiful things when you understand the scriptures correctly, and this includes the Torah, is the Torah, in addition to all these commandments and all of these beautiful truths that we don't live up to."],[2219.17,"Maybe starting with this one."],[2220.91,"That we don't live up to, also has the whole process of what to do when you don't live up to them."],[2230.11,"Repentance. Returning, healing."],[2233.9,"There's no myth here that we're gonna live up to things, or that most people, or that much of anybody, is gonna manage to perfectly live up to this."],[2242.15,"Because it remains that goal, that telos."],[2247.21,"There's nobody on earth who has hit that destiny."],[2255.69,"There isn't. That includes the saints, by the way."],[2258.33,"And you know who would be the first to tell you that?"],[2260.65,"The saints. St."],[2262.71,"Sisoes the Great, monastic saint."],[2266.47,"When he was dying, he's one of the desert fathers."],[2268.95,"He was laying there dying, and as he was dying, his light was shining with the uncreated lig,ht his face was shining. with the uncreated light of God that his disciples saw"],[2282.43,"And his disciples said to him, Do you have any last words for us, Father?"],[2287.71,"And he said, I regret that I had only just started to repent of my sins."],[2296.48,"So built into this idea of potential of destiny is that we're not gonna achieve it, but we press toward it continually."],[2306.58,"And we work toward it."],[2308.5,"And so, yes, we haven't lived up to this."],[2315.46,"And we're gonna see when we get to Genesis 3, the quote unquote curse, and we'll talk about what that actually means with Adam and Eve, even though she wasn't Eve yet."],[2327.1,"But with Adam and Eve, part of that curse is that they're gonna be at odds with each other."],[2337.07,"But we'll talk about that more in chapter two, that they're created to cooperate."],[2340.77,"And work together and instead"],[2350.94,"So, yes, that short sentence there, putting in the male and female, is huge, and we read right over it."],[2359.23,"But is uh massive?"],[2363.35,"Um so now notice the command that they're given."],[2370.79,"Notice that they're given it has said to them be fruitful and multiply, fill the earth and subdue it, and have dominion over the fish of the sea, over the birds of heaven, and over every living thing that moves on the earth."],[2387.19,"So there's two pieces there."],[2389.83,"Fill the earth, subdue it."],[2392.55,"And the word that's translated subdue is a word that's elsewhere used to kind of conquer."],[2398.17,"To take control of something."],[2402.33,"Do those two things remind you of anything?"],[2406.21,"This is the text that people use to justify polluting the whole planet."],[2410.45,"Yeah, yeah. He doesn't say pollute anything."],[2416.41,"These are the two things God's been doing so far this chapter."],[2420.41,"In reverse order."],[2422.57,"Fill the earth with life."],[2427.34,"Subdue it. Put it in order."],[2430.4,"Give order to it."],[2433.92,"So part of that imaging, doing what God does, is that we're to continu,e we were to continue the work of creation."],[2443.76,"Because as we're going to see next time when we get into chapter 2, Eden. is not, paradise is not the whole world"],[2455.24,"It's in the world."],[2457.8,"And so humanity was supposed to go out and sort of continue that work."],[2465.16,"That's part of what it means to image God."],[2467.64,"And so there's those two pieces."],[2468.76,"But we'll talk about that more when we get into chapter two."],[2471.88,"And yeah, that exercising dominion is not massacre."],[2477.1,"It's not, it's not create order in the Roman sense of create a wasteland and call it peace."],[2487.12,"Um, because those two things have to go together."],[2491.52,"That kind of order, the order represented by like tyranny"],[2497.48,"Does not bring forth life."],[2503.0,"This is why when we get into chapter two, why it's a garden."],[2507.48,"Because a garden is sort of the perfect con combination of those two ideas."],[2513.16,"It's put in order, and because it is in good order, it brings forth life in abundance."],[2519.72,"So a parking lot can be nice and orderly, but there's no life."],[2525.41,"And our local swamps are full of life, but they're not in order, and that makes them dangerous."],[2535.63,"And so both."],[2542.0,"So verse 29."],[2543.12,"Then God said, Behold, I have given you every seed-bearing herb that sows seed on the face of all the earth, and every tree whose fruit yields seed."],[2553.92,"To you it shall be for food."],[2556.48,"I also give every green plant as food for all the wild animals of the earth, for all the birds of heaven, and for everything that creeps on the earth, in which is the breath of life."],[2565.92,"It was so."],[2567.52,"Then God saw everything he had made, and indeed it was very good."],[2571.36,"So evening and morning were the sixth day."],[2576.32,"So in the parallel between day three and day six, notice there's even the two pieces."],[2582.84,"Remember the two things that happened on day three?"],[2586.68,"Were the dry land and then all the plants."],[2591.18,"So we have the creatures, and then they're given the plants."],[2594.14,"So that's why the plants are there."],[2595.5,"The plants are there to feed."],[2599.38,"All of the living creatures."],[2600.82,"But notice how the plants are described and the focus on fruit from trees."],[2607.72,"So this sort of original diet here does not involve killing anything."],[2614.36,"Doesn't even really involve killing plants."],[2618.28,"The sea-bearing herbs we're talking about are like wheat."],[2622.96,"So the idea here is that in this original order of creation, the creation offers itself to the living creatures, including humanity, in the world for sustenance."],[2635.66,"That's part of this order."],[2639.34,"And we don't see, and the animals are also eating plants in this."],[2644.7,"And this is the idea that there's not this predatory thing happening."],[2650.14,"And we'll see how that kind of devolves as we go forward."],[2655.66,"Yeah, we'll do day seven"],[2660.22,"I've gone a little long, but we'll do day seven."],[2662.94,"Because I feel weird leaving off there, even though there is a chapter break."],[2666.7,"This is the we one of the weirdest chapter breaks of the Bible."],[2666.94,"the Like, why didn't you make break before verse four?"],[2675.38,"Yeah. But so chapter two, verse one."],[2680.98,"Thus heaven and earth and all their adornment were finished."],[2684.44,"So notice heaven and earth, adornment, spaces filled."],[2692.73,"And on the seventh day, God finished the works he made, and he rested on the seventh day from all the works he made."],[2700.25,"Then God blessed the seventh day and sanctified it, because in it he rested from all his works God began to make."],[2708.17,"So a couple of pieces with that"],[2714.93,"Notice he rested from all the works that he began to make."],[2723.85,"Is there a switch in verbiage there from the create verb to the make verb?"],[2728.97,"No, it's the same, it's the same verb."],[2731.13,"They've been using make all along in the Orthodox Study Bible."],[2734.89,"Um, but began."],[2737.63,"Christ is actually going to reference this."],[2740.19,"One of the umpteen times that the Pharisees come and pester he and his disciples for doing something on the Sabbath in St."],[2747.87,"John's Gospel. They're picking heads of wheat."],[2752.53,"On the Sabbath."],[2753.49,"And they come"],[2756.17,"Why are you doing this?"],[2758.01,"And Christ responds and says that God is working, his father's working, even to that very day."],[2766.22,"So this is neither, as say, the Thomas Jeffersons among us would have us believe, that God gets finishes the six days, and then it just walks off somewhere."],[2780.0,"Hands off now, everything's just going to chug along of its own accord."],[2783.52,"It's also not God taking a break for 24 hours."],[2787.44,"And then getting back to work."],[2789.85,"Because that doesn't make a whole lot of sense."],[2793.9,"What's what's uh actually going on here?"],[2797.94,"Well, so we have the word rest, and rest the way we use it in English, brings this image to mind of whew."],[2809.8,"I need to sit down and drink some non-alcoholic iced tea because there's kids here, and uh cool off and relax."],[2819.9,"Now, obviously, that doesn't apply to God."],[2823.95,"But rest has another and more common earlier meaning."],[2830.48,"Like a rock rolls down a hill and comes to rest somewhere."],[2837.01,"What does that mean?"],[2839.53,"It comes to the place where it sits."],[2843.34,"And so what's actually being gotten at here, the image here is of God sitting down."],[2850.94,"He's not just sitting down somewhere to take a break."],[2853.98,"This is God enthroned now over his creation."],[2860.46,"So God's rest is another way of talking about God's kingdom."],[2869.12,"Everything is now in order."],[2872.72,"Meaning there's justice, there's peace."],[2876.32,"He sits at his throne, to preside over his creation."],[2881.3,"And so the Sabbath, when he talks about hallowing that day, and this is, of course, we mentioned that there are all these places in Genesis that refer to things that are going to be commandments later on in the Torah."],[2893.03,"This is Exhibit A."],[2894.81,"The Sabbath day and the Sabbath year."],[2896.97,"They're going to be commanded later."],[2899.21,"That rest, is at the end of the week."],[2904.14,"It's the last day of the week."],[2906.46,"And the idea is that on that day they are entering into God's rest."],[2911.82,"They're entering into his kingdom, into his rule."],[2917.2,"And that's why they're not to busy themselves about work."],[2923.52,"They're to enjoy."],[2924.56,"And so the idea was this is looking forward"],[2925.5,"The kingdom. This was the hope at the end of your life in this world, was that you would go to rest."],[2939.34,"We're going to see later in Genesis, when Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, when they die, and they're buried, it's going to say that they went to rest."],[2952.86,"They entered into his kingdom"],[2957.34,"This is at the end."],[2960.15,"And God is enthroned to rule."],[2964.71,"Now, when we say that Christ fulfilled the Sabbath, what do we mean?"],[2973.07,"On the sixth day of the week, on Friday, he finished all his work."],[2978.51,"And in St."],[2979.39,"John's Gospel, what's usually translated in English as it is finished, those are his last words, or his last word, it's one word in Greek."],[2990.83,"On the cross."],[2992.27,"That's the same verb that's used here for God finishing his work."],[2999.79,"So Christ finishes his work, and then what does he do?"],[3004.11,"He rests in the tomb on the Sabbath."],[3007.87,"And then he rises again."],[3011.55,"New week. eighth day"],[3015.27,"We'll get into eighth day a little later in Genesis when we talk about people being circumcised on the eighth day."],[3023.35,"The new creation starts."],[3028.92,"So in the old testament with the Sabbath, they're looking forward to the end, the purpose, the goal of the first creation."],[3046.2,"We worship and celebrate on the Lord's day, the first day of the week."],[3052.46,"The day of Christ's resurrection, because we're looking to at celebrating the beginning of the new creation."],[3057.34,"And so it moves"],[3064.5,"In that way."],[3067.22,"But so that's that's that concept of rest."],[3070.92,"And when it's talking about the deaths of the patriarchs and those kind of things."],[3077.4,"So unless anybody has any questions that they want in the recording, we'll end here."],[3082.6,"And we'll pick up in uh chapter two, verse four, where we get the first, this is the book of the generations that I told you about."],[3090.8,"Uh in chapter two, verse four."],[3093.36,"Uh, next week, Lord will it."],[3095.0,"So thank you, everybody."],[3097.56,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3102.04,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaithcom."],[3110.24,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.48,"lv":2,"h":"Genesis 1:9-13 - Day three: dry land and plants","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.9</b>: waters under heaven <b>gathered into one place</b>, dry land appears</li><li class=\"d1\">God calls dry land <b>earth</b>, gathered waters <b>seas</b> - \"it was good\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.11</b>: earth brings forth <b>herb of grass</b> with seed, <b>fruit tree</b> with seed in itself, <b>according to its kind</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"evening and morning were the <b>third day</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Notice the language</li><li class=\"d1\">God doesn't make land <b>rise up out of the water</b> - how a movie would show it</li><li class=\"d1\">he <b>pulls the waters back and restrains them</b>, so dry land appears</li><li class=\"d0\">Why? <b>Seas = chaos</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we started with waters, chaos</li><li class=\"d1\">God <b>restrains the chaos</b> to produce <b>order</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he holds chaos \"out at the extremes\" back from the ordered earth</li><li class=\"d0\">Transition: spaces made, now <b>beginnings of life</b></li></ul>"},{"t":144.31,"lv":3,"h":"Life begins; two sets of three days","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Plants and <b>fruit trees</b> = first life</li><li class=\"d1\">fruit by definition has <b>seeds</b> - \"a plant ovum\" - can reproduce</li><li class=\"d0\">First three days = fixing the first problem: <b>disorder, chaos</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God putting things in order, creating <b>spaces</b> - still mostly <b>empty</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Next three days <b>parallel</b> the first three - answering the second problem: <b>emptiness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two parallel sets of three days - important to the <b>fathers</b> and to our <b>liturgy</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Holy Saturday</b> reading before <i>Pascha</i> = <b>Genesis 1</b>, first three days only</li><li class=\"d1\">\"why stop halfway through the story?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">tied to <b>Christ rising on the third day</b></li><li class=\"d1\">more about Christ and the <b>Sabbath</b> at the end of the chapter</li><li class=\"d0\">Connection: <b>restoration of order</b></li><li class=\"d1\">order later called <b>justice</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Paul</b> (Romans): Christ raised for our <b>justification</b></li><li class=\"d1\">justified = <b>put back in order</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ's third day = restoring order of creation - Revelation: \"<b>making all things new</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":315.99,"lv":2,"h":"1:14-19 - Day four: sun, moon, stars","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.14</b>: lights in the firmament <b>for illumination</b>, to <b>divide day from night</b></li><li class=\"d1\">two great lights: greater <b>rules the day</b>, lesser <b>rules the night</b>; <b>stars</b> also</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>good</b>\" - fourth day</li><li class=\"d0\">Now filling the ordered spaces with <b>life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why take day four and day one <b>super literally</b> = problems (light before sun)</li><li class=\"d0\">We don't think of sun, moon, stars as <b>life</b> - modern view</li><li class=\"d1\">sun = incandescent gas, <b>nuclear furnace</b>, hydrogen to helium</li><li class=\"d1\">moon = <b>big rock</b> orbiting; stars like the sun far away; planets like the moon</li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient people: not a <b>representation</b> of divine beings - they <b>were</b> those beings</li><li class=\"d1\">scripture: <b>angelic beings</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Revelation: <b>angels are stars</b> all the time - \"direct comparison\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Could be plasma and rocks that <b>divide seasons, times, years</b> - but the point is the <b>ruling</b></li></ul>"},{"t":488.1,"lv":3,"h":"Angelic administration","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why many <b>fathers</b> link day four to the <b>creation of the angels</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Philo</b> (1st c. BC Jewish writer): angels <b>not gods</b> - angelic beings who <b>serve God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God has an <b>administration</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he's the <b>king</b>; the king has a <b>court</b></li><li class=\"d1\">angels assigned to parts of creation to <b>oversee and guide</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Later in Torah: sun, moon, stars given <b>governance of the nations other than Israel</b> - <b>Deut 4</b> - \"what?\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Iconography</b>: sun and moon with <b>faces</b></li><li class=\"d1\">at the crucifixion, sun and moon <b>hiding their faces</b></li><li class=\"d0\">OT promise (Abraham): the <b>righteous will shine like stars</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sainthood</b> = glorified humans take up places in that administration</li><li class=\"d0\">God doesn't <b>need</b> help - not \"too big, needs a bureaucracy\"</li><li class=\"d1\">out of <b>love</b> he <b>shares his life and rule</b> with creatures, angels included</li><li class=\"d0\">Light/darkness, day/night, heavens = creatures who live there - day four parallels <b>day one</b></li></ul>"},{"t":647.36,"lv":2,"h":"1:20-23 - Day five: sea and sky; \"every soul\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.20</b>: waters bring forth <b>creatures having life</b>; birds fly across the firmament</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>great sea creatures</b>, every living thing that moves, every winged bird <b>according to its kind</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>blessing</b>: \"Be fruitful and multiply, fill the waters and the seas\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Parallel: <b>day two</b> separated sky and sea; <b>day five</b> fills them with life, tells them to continue</li><li class=\"d1\">makes more sense than birds coming out of the sea</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew: \"every living thing that moves\" = literally <b>every soul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Western <b>Platonic</b> habit: humans have souls, nothing else</li><li class=\"d1\">ancients: <b>having a soul = alive</b>, not inanimate</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>anima</i> = Latin soul; <b>inanimate = soulless</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Different living things, <b>different kinds of souls</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Gregory Palamas</b>: treatise on the <b>souls of animals</b> - \"my dogs could be heathens\"</li><li class=\"d1\">animals have souls, but <b>not a human soul</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Translators softened \"souls that swim in the waters\" to \"living things\" - fair, same meaning</li></ul>"},{"t":822.34,"lv":2,"h":"1:24-25 - Day six: land animals","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.24</b>: earth brings forth living creatures <b>according to its kind</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>quadrupeds</b> (\"great translation\"), <b>creeping things</b>, <b>wild animals</b>, <b>cattle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"God saw that it was <b>good</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Pause before humans: \"Then God said, Let us...\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Parallel again: <b>third day</b> made dry land; <b>sixth day</b> fills it with life</li><li class=\"d0\">Humans come next</li></ul>"},{"t":871.44,"lv":3,"h":"\"God saw that it was good\" = tov","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">English \"good\" has many uses</li><li class=\"d1\">good vs bad/evil</li><li class=\"d1\">mild praise: \"tastes good\", \"<b>home of good boudin</b>\" sign - \"a modest claim\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>none</b> of these is the point here</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew <i>tov</i> = more like <b>beautiful or whole</b></li><li class=\"d1\">beauty = <b>order</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not only days 1-3: days five and six bring animals <b>according to their kinds</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Interesting juxtaposition</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sea creatures</b> - sea = chaos - incl. <b>sea monsters</b>, \"according to their kind\"</li><li class=\"d1\">land: <b>creeping things</b> = reptiles (<i>herpeton</i> in Greek)</li><li class=\"d1\">animals seen as <b>unclean</b>, indicative of <b>chaos</b> - still \"according to their kind\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So even these are part of <b>God's order</b> in the original creation</li><li class=\"d1\">can be called good = <b>beauty and wholeness</b>, because of that order</li></ul>"},{"t":1032.64,"lv":2,"h":"1:26-28 - \"Let us make man\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.26</b>: \"Let us make man in our <b>image</b>, according to our <b>likeness</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>dominion</b> over fish, birds, cattle, all the earth, every creeping thing</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>v.27</b>: in the <b>image of God</b> he made him - <b>male and female</b> he made them</li><li class=\"d1\">blessed: be fruitful, multiply, fill the earth, <b>subdue</b> it, have dominion</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Let us\" - \"I'm probably gonna get accused of heresy\"</li><li class=\"d1\">many want to connect it to the <b>Trinity</b> - \"but this is not the place\"</li><li class=\"d1\">grammatical point in a minute</li><li class=\"d0\">Trinity in Genesis 1 - <b>already seen</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God creating; <b>Spirit of God hovering</b> over the waters</li><li class=\"d1\">creates by his <b>Word</b>: \"let there be light\"</li><li class=\"d1\">creation through <b>word and Spirit</b> = the Trinity</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. John</b>: \"all things were made through him\" - word becomes flesh = <b>Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1166.08,"lv":3,"h":"<i>Elohim</i>: plural of intensification","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><i>Elohim</i>, translated \"God\" all through, is <b>grammatically plural</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Some say <b>plural of majesty</b> - the <b>royal we</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Queen Victoria \"we are not amused\" - \"in every movie she says that\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not totally off base, not right: Hebrew has <b>no plural of majesty</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew does have a <b>plural of intensification</b></li><li class=\"d1\">English \"mountain of mountains\" / \"great mountain\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew: just make it plural - <i>harim</i> for <b>one mountain</b> = \"the mountain of mountains\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>kings</b>\" instead of king of kings</li><li class=\"d0\">Biblical example: <b>Behemoth</b></li><li class=\"d1\">grammatical plural of the word for <b>cow or bull</b> - \"super cow\"</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>beast of beasts</b> - talked about in Revelation</li><li class=\"d0\">Elohim = <b>intensifier</b>: God of gods, the great God, <b>God most high</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1325.72,"lv":3,"h":"Why not singular <i>El</i>","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Extra reason: singular <i>El</i> also means God</li><li class=\"d1\">but <i>El</i> was also the name of a <b>pagan god</b>, <b>father of Baal</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"In the beginning <i>El</i> created...\" - Canaanites: \"when does he <b>take a wife and give birth to Baal</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Plural <i>Elohim</i> distinguishes the God here from that god</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>God capital G</b>, the most high God - not that dude\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Because plural, you sometimes find <b>plural pronouns and verbs</b> - \"let us\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"I <b>believe</b> in the Trinity\" - but this is a <b>bad argument</b> for it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not going to convince much of anybody\" - don't take it too far</li><li class=\"d0\">Better: God creating through his <b>word and Spirit</b>; connect to the beginning of <b>St. John's Gospel</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1445.74,"lv":2,"h":"What \"image of God\" means","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.26-27: \"in our image\" - God does it</li><li class=\"d0\">We think of image as a <b>static concept</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not woodenly literal: <b>image like photo</b>, \"we look like God\" - \"unless you're <b>Kenneth Copeland</b>\" (\"cheap shot\")</li><li class=\"d1\">not physical appearance</li><li class=\"d0\">Common move: pick a <b>faculty</b> and say that's the image - <b>super problematic</b></li><li class=\"d1\">many don't work out</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>language</b> - Christ is the Word - but lots of animals have language</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>reason</b> - from <b>Aristotle</b> more than the Bible</li><li class=\"d0\">Reason causes a problem</li><li class=\"d1\">unborn infant has no capacity to reason - not in God's image?</li><li class=\"d1\">advanced <b>dementia</b>, <b>brain injury</b> - no longer human in the image?</li><li class=\"d0\">Nailing it to a faculty = doesn't work out, and <b>dangerous morally and ethically</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not hypothetical - <b>has happened</b> in human history</li></ul>"},{"t":1606.56,"lv":3,"h":"Image as dynamic capacity","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Concept is much more <b>dynamic than static</b></li><li class=\"d0\">It's a <b>capacity, a potential</b> humans have</li><li class=\"d1\">imaging God = something we can <b>actively do or not do</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>functioning</b> as God's image</li><li class=\"d0\">Includes <b>Father and Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">NT: Christ = <b>express image</b>, perfect, complete image of the Father</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. John</b>: what he says = what the Father says; what he does = what the Father does</li><li class=\"d0\">Like \"you're the <b>spitting image of your dad</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes physical appearance</li><li class=\"d1\">more often <b>how I talk</b>, a mannerism, a way of expressing myself - \"reminds them\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1717.68,"lv":3,"h":"Image vs. likeness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Image and likeness\" - Hebrew and Greek words are basically <b>synonyms</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fathers still make a <b>distinction</b> - not grammatical</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Image</b> = the <b>potential</b> we have</li><li class=\"d1\">to be <b>imitators of God</b>, <b>participants</b>, his image in the world, <b>reflect him</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Likeness</b> = the <b>end, the goal</b></li><li class=\"d1\">final fulfillment we aim toward - fullness of the image</li><li class=\"d1\">through being his image in the world we <b>become like him</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Image = something we're <b>born possessing</b> as potential</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Gregory the Theologian</b>: humans are the being \"commanded to <b>become God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">This <b>destiny and potentiality</b> separates humans from other creatures - <b>not just animals, but angels</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Fulfilled in <b>Christ</b>: humanity and God <b>perfectly united</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not incarnate as a dog, a sheep, or an angel - but our <b>human nature</b></li><li class=\"d1\">that gives us the potential and destiny</li></ul>"},{"t":1877.47,"lv":2,"h":"1:27 - Male and female","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Male and female he created them\" - very important; creation of man and woman again in <b>chapter 2</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Obvious to us now - for most of history <b>radical, revolutionary</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ancient Near East: hard to find anyone who thinks <b>women are human</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Aristotle</b> (4th c. BC): women = <b>defective men</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all fetuses developing into men; some \"don't quite get there\" - <b>birth defect</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"an improvement over a lot of other cultures\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Plato</b>, the <i>Symposium</i>: a man can't love a woman - only loves his <b>equal</b></li><li class=\"d0\">This is the whole ancient world - and not a NT thing, <b>the Torah already has this</b></li><li class=\"d1\">male and female on an <b>equal plane</b> - in <b>potential and destiny</b>, serving as the image of God</li><li class=\"d1\">\"unheard of\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2007.94,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: later attitudes; Ben Sira","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: where did that evolve? - side comments: Jewish saying thanking God \"I'm not a <b>woman</b>... <b>Gentile</b>\" - \"that's later\"</li><li class=\"d0\">You do find such things later</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Ben Sira</b> says outright at one point <b>\"women are evil\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">trying to do something with <b>Eve</b> - giving <b>Adam's excuse</b> (Gen 3)</li><li class=\"d0\">Adam's excuse didn't get him far</li><li class=\"d1\">God questions him: \"it's not me, it's <b>this woman you put here with me</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Ben Sira still tried to use it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Paul</b> responds to that in <b>Ephesians</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2090.59,"lv":2,"h":"Israel's failure; hypocrite vs. heretic","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Rest of the OT = <b>Israel's failure to keep the Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"God gave the Torah, they didn't keep it - that's it\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Paul</b>, <b>Romans 2</b>: great advantage to being Jewish - you have the Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">but do you <b>keep</b> it? If not, not a big advantage</li><li class=\"d0\">Contemporary objection: ancient Israel, Second Temple Jews, Christians haven't lived up to the Bible → \"the Bible must not actually say that\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"no no no\" - you don't decide what the Bible or Christianity teaches by <b>what we've pulled off as a mass group</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not individual <b>saints</b> who may have - \"people like me, who don't\"</li><li class=\"d1\">you look at <b>what it says</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Worse things to be than a <b>hypocrite</b> - at least you have <b>standards</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>better a hypocrite than a heretic</b> - \"you can quote me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">at least pursuing the <b>right goal</b>, falling short</li></ul>"},{"t":2205.31,"lv":3,"h":"The Torah includes repentance; St. Sisoes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Beauty of the scriptures, Torah included</li><li class=\"d1\">commandments and truths we <b>don't live up to</b> - \"maybe starting with this one\"</li><li class=\"d1\">also the <b>whole process for when you don't</b>: <b>repentance</b>, returning, <b>healing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No myth we'll live up to it - it remains the goal, the <i>telos</i></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>nobody on earth</b> has hit that destiny - <b>saints included</b>; they'd be first to say so</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Sisoes the Great</b>, desert father</li><li class=\"d1\">dying, face shining with the <b>uncreated light</b> of God, disciples saw it</li><li class=\"d1\">last words? \"I regret that I had only <b>just started to repent</b> of my sins\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Built into potential and destiny: we won't achieve it, but <b>press toward it continually</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Preview, <b>Genesis 3</b>: the \"quote unquote <b>curse</b>\" - Adam and Eve (\"she wasn't Eve yet\")</li><li class=\"d1\">part of it: <b>at odds with each other</b></li><li class=\"d1\">created to <b>cooperate and work together</b> - more in ch. 2</li><li class=\"d0\">Short phrase \"male and female\" = <b>huge</b> - \"we read right over it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2363.35,"lv":2,"h":"1:28 - Fill the earth, subdue it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">The command: <b>be fruitful and multiply, fill the earth, subdue it</b>, have dominion</li><li class=\"d0\">Two pieces: <b>fill</b> the earth; <b>subdue</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>subdue</i> elsewhere = <b>conquer</b>, take control</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Remind you of anything?\" - text used to justify <b>polluting the planet</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he doesn't say pollute anything</li><li class=\"d0\">These are the <b>two things God's been doing</b> this chapter - in reverse order</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>fill</b> the earth with <b>life</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>subdue</b> = put it in <b>order</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Part of imaging God: we were to <b>continue the work of creation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ch. 2: <b>Eden</b>, paradise, is <b>not the whole world</b> - it's in the world</li><li class=\"d1\">humanity to go out and continue that work - more in chapter two</li></ul>"},{"t":2471.88,"lv":3,"h":"Dominion is not massacre","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Exercising dominion is <b>not massacre</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not order in the Roman sense: \"create a <b>wasteland</b> and call it <b>peace</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Two things must go together</li><li class=\"d1\">order of <b>tyranny</b> does <b>not bring forth life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why ch. 2 is a <b>garden</b></li><li class=\"d1\">perfect combination: <b>in order</b>, so it brings forth <b>life in abundance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Examples</li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>parking lot</b>: orderly, no life</li><li class=\"d1\">local <b>swamps</b>: full of life, not in order - <b>dangerous</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"So both\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2535.63,"lv":2,"h":"1:29-31 - Plants as food; day six ends","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.29</b>: every <b>seed-bearing herb</b> and every <b>tree whose fruit yields seed</b> - for <b>food</b></li><li class=\"d1\">every green plant for wild animals, birds, creeping things \"in which is the <b>breath of life</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"God saw everything he had made, and indeed it was <b>very good</b>\" - <b>sixth day</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Parallel <b>day three / day six</b>: two things on day three - <b>dry land</b>, then <b>plants</b></li><li class=\"d1\">day six: the <b>creatures</b>, then given the plants</li><li class=\"d1\">plants are there to <b>feed</b> all the living creatures</li><li class=\"d0\">Notice focus on <b>fruit from trees</b></li><li class=\"d1\">original diet involves <b>no killing</b> - not even really plants</li><li class=\"d1\">seed-bearing herbs like <b>wheat</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Original order: creation <b>offers itself</b> to living creatures, humanity included, for <b>sustenance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">animals eat plants too - <b>no predatory thing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we'll see how that kind of <b>devolves</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2655.66,"lv":2,"h":"2:1-3 - Day seven","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Gone a little long\" - but \"weird leaving off there\"</li><li class=\"d1\">one of the <b>weirdest chapter breaks</b> - why not break before <b>2:4</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:1</b>: heaven and earth and all their <b>adornment</b> finished</li><li class=\"d1\">spaces <b>filled</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.2-3</b>: seventh day God <b>finished</b> his works, <b>rested</b>; <b>blessed</b> and <b>sanctified</b> the seventh day</li><li class=\"d1\">\"rested from all the works he <b>began to make</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same verb as before - Orthodox Study Bible has \"make\" all along</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ references this in <b>St. John's Gospel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one of the umpteen times Pharisees <b>pester</b> him over the <b>Sabbath</b> - picking <b>heads of wheat</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ: \"my Father is <b>working</b>, even to this very day\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not the <b>Thomas Jeffersons</b>: God finishes, walks off, <b>hands off</b>, it chugs along</li><li class=\"d0\">Not God taking a <b>24-hour break</b> then back to work - \"doesn't make much sense\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2793.9,"lv":3,"h":"Rest = God enthroned","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Rest\" in English: \"whew... sit down with <b>non-alcoholic iced tea</b>\" - doesn't apply to God</li><li class=\"d0\">Earlier, more common meaning: a <b>rock rolls down a hill and comes to rest</b></li><li class=\"d1\">comes to the place where it <b>sits</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Image: <b>God sitting down</b> - not a break</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>God enthroned</b> over his creation</li><li class=\"d1\">rest = another way of talking about <b>God's kingdom</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Everything in order = <b>justice, peace</b>; he sits at his <b>throne</b> to preside</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sabbath</b>: hallowing the day</li><li class=\"d1\">Genesis refers to things later <b>commanded in the Torah</b> - \"this is <b>Exhibit A</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Sabbath day</b> and <b>Sabbath year</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Rest comes at the <b>end of the week</b></li><li class=\"d1\">on that day they <b>enter into God's rest, his kingdom, his rule</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why they don't busy themselves with work - they <b>enjoy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">looking forward to <b>the kingdom</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2964.71,"lv":3,"h":"Christ fulfills the Sabbath; next week","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hope at the end of life: you would <b>go to rest</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Abraham, Isaac, Jacob</b> die, are buried: \"they went to rest\" - <b>entered his kingdom</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"God is enthroned to rule\"</li><li class=\"d0\">How Christ <b>fulfilled the Sabbath</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sixth day</b>, Friday: finished all his work</li><li class=\"d1\">St. John: \"<b>it is finished</b>\" = <b>one word in Greek</b>, same verb as God finishing his work here</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>rests in the tomb</b> on the Sabbath</li><li class=\"d1\">rises again: <b>new week, eighth day</b> - circumcision on the eighth day later in Genesis</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>new creation</b> starts</li><li class=\"d0\">Old Testament Sabbath: looking forward to the <b>end, purpose, goal</b> of the first creation</li><li class=\"d0\">We worship on the <b>Lord's day</b>, first day of the week</li><li class=\"d1\">day of the <b>resurrection</b> - celebrating the <b>beginning of the new creation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Concept of <b>rest</b> - also behind the deaths of the patriarchs</li><li class=\"d0\">No questions; ends here</li></ul>"}],"next":"Next week: <b>Genesis 2:4</b> - \"the book of the generations\""});
